  "articleBody": "Homeowners dealing with a sticking bathroom door often wonder whether a quick repair will do the trick or if a full frame replacement is necessary. Choosing the right path saves money and prevents recurrent moisture troubles.\n\nKey Differences\nA partial repair involves tightening loose hinge screws, splicing in a new lower jamb segment, or sanding and filling superficial gouges. A full replacement involves pulling out the entire jamb, shimming a fresh frame plumb against the rough studs, and hanging the slab anew.\n\nWhen to Choose Each\nChoose a partial repair when the wood remains structurally sound, the door slab hangs plumb, and damage is limited to hardware loosening or small cosmetic dings. Choose full replacement when water has rotted the lower jamb, the frame has racked out of square, or moisture has compromised the sill and rough opening studs. Attempting to patch soft, moisture-swollen wood usually results in recurring latch failures.\n\nCost and Timeline Tradeoffs\nA basic jamb tune-up or minor splice typically wraps up in a few hours with minimal material costs. Complete frame replacement takes longer because it requires pulling casing, resetting shims, reinstalling trim, and repainting. However, replacing a ruined frame prevents repeated repair calls down the road.\n\nIf you are unsure whether your bathroom door jamb needs a splice or a complete rebuild, call HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 to discuss your project with Abraham Becerra.",
